<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # <span style="color: #000;">What Are the Jobs of the Future?</span> – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Dream Jobs</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Are the Jobs of the Future?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain future career or educational plans. Interactive: Participate in role-plays about job interviews.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain to peers the required skills for a future job (e.g., "You need to be good at math to work in engineering."). Can participate in a role-play to practice for a job interview, asking and answering detailed questions about strengths, goals, and required skills (e.g., "What is your greatest strength?" "I am a quick learner, and I enjoy working with technology."). Can share future educational plans and reasons (e.g., "I will study engineering to work on technology projects.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f8ff;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of futuristic jobs (e.g., AI ethicist, virtual reality designer, drone operator, renewable energy specialist) and asks students, "What do you see?" "What skills do you think these future jobs will require?" "Will traditional jobs disappear, or will they evolve?" Students discuss in pairs for 2 minutes, then share one idea with the class, activating vocabulary and ideas from previous lessons.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using future simple, future continuous, and modals for prediction and possibility. E.g., "Many jobs will involve technology." "In ten years, people will be working remotely more often." "Jobs may require strong adaptability." The teacher also models the pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "automation," "advancement," "adaptability," and contrastive sounds such as /dʒ/ in "change," "job" and /ʒ/ in "vision," "leisure," ensuring clear articulation for speaking focus.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'Jobs may require new skills,' does it mean they *definitely* need new skills, or it's a *possibility*?" (Expected answer: A possibility).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'Tomorrow, I will be working on a project,' am I talking about a finished action or an ongoing action in the future?" (Expected answer: An ongoing action).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Dream Jobs."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a short mock job interview (around 1.5-2 minutes) where the interviewer asks about the candidate's future career plans, strengths, and how they anticipate the job market will evolve. The dialogue incorporates the target grammar (future simple, future continuous, modals for possibility) and vocabulary (e.g., "in-demand skills," "adaptability," "automation," "innovation," "remote work").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can understand the main points of clear, standard speech on familiar matters regularly encountered in work, school, leisure, etc., including short social exchanges and routine interview questions about future plans.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question (First Listening):</b> "What is the main topic of the conversation?" (Expected answer: A job interview about future careers and skills.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed questions (Second Listening):</b>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">According to the candidate, what kind of industry will be growing in the future?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific skill does the candidate mention they will be developing?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What does the candidate say about remote work?</li>
                    </ol>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice expressing future predictions and plans using future simple (will/won't), future continuous (will be + -ing), and modals for possibility (may/might/could require). Key vocabulary includes: <i>future, demand, advancement, automation, remote work, career, technology, innovation, adaptability, acquire, require, evolve, develop, foresee, in-demand, adaptable, evolving</i>. Practice of /dʒ/ in "change," "job" and /ʒ/ in "vision," and stress in "automation," "advancement."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> Teacher provides sentence beginnings on the board or a handout, and students complete them orally with a partner, using the target grammar and vocabulary. E.g., "In the next five years, I will be ______ (study engineering / acquiring new skills)." "Future jobs ______ (require technological adaptability / disappear due to automation)." "My dream career ______ (will involve innovation / may demand constant learning)." Teacher monitors for correct grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation of key sounds and word stress.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Future Job Skills" Chain Drill (Semi-controlled):</b> Students sit in a circle. The first student starts by saying, "In the future, a [job] will require [skill]." (e.g., "In the future, an AI specialist will require adaptability."). The next student adds to the sentence or creates a new one, incorporating future continuous or a modal, building on the previous idea or introducing a new job/skill. (e.g., "Next, people will be working with artificial intelligence, which may demand creativity."). Teacher prompts for specific grammar if needed and corrects pronunciation for /dʒ/ and /ʒ/ (e.g., "change," "vision").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Interview Practice (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Partner A asks Partner B questions about their future career plans using prompts: "What careers do you think will be in demand?" "What skills will you be developing for your chosen field?" "What challenges might you foresee?" Partner B answers, focusing on using future simple, future continuous, modals, and target vocabulary. Then they switch roles. Students listen to each other and provide polite, constructive feedback on clarity and use of target language.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Dream Jobs."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play – "Future Job Interview." Students will work in pairs. One student acts as a job interviewer for a futuristic company, and the other is a candidate applying for a "dream job of the future" (e.g., sustainable technology engineer, virtual reality experience designer, data ethicist). The interviewer will ask detailed questions about the candidate's future educational plans, career goals, required skills, and how they anticipate the job market will evolve. The candidate must explain their plans, strengths, and how they will adapt to future challenges.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Role Assignment:</b> Students divide into pairs (Interviewer/Candidate).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Scenario Planning (5 min):</b> Each pair quickly plans their futuristic job and company. Candidates think about their "dream job" and how they will explain their educational plans (e.g., "I will study engineering to work on innovative projects.") and required skills (e.g., "You need to be good at math to work in engineering."). Interviewers prepare 3-4 questions, using pragmatics like "What careers do you think will be in demand in your chosen field?" and sociolinguistic awareness to adjust formality (e.g., using "I would like to know..." instead of "Tell me...").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Role-Play (8-10 min per pair):</b> Students perform their interview. They must use future simple, future continuous, and modals for possibility. They should incorporate as many target vocabulary words as possible (e.g., "automation," "adaptability," "in-demand," "evolve"). They are encouraged to practice stress on multisyllabic words and distinguish /dʒ/ and /ʒ/ sounds.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>4. Switch Roles (8-10 min per pair):</b> Students swap roles and repeat the interview.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, communicative task completion. Students should be able to explain their future plans clearly and interact confidently, asking and answering detailed questions about strengths, goals, and required skills, adapting their tone and language choices based on the audience (interviewer/peer).</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on the use of target grammar, vocabulary, pronunciation, and interaction during the role-play. Peer feedback forms will also be used.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Future Job Interview" role-play in their pairs. The teacher circulates and observes, making notes on the checklist. Afterwards, each student provides one piece of positive feedback and one suggestion for improvement to their partner using a simple peer feedback form.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of contrastive sounds (/dʒ/, /ʒ/) and stress in multisyllabic words (e.g., "automation," "adaptability").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate and consistent use of future simple, future continuous, and modals for possibility.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Relevant and accurate use of target vocabulary (nouns, verbs, adjectives related to jobs and future trends).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in explaining personal career goals and required skills.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active and collaborative interaction, asking and answering detailed questions.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Adaptation of tone and formality based on the role-play context (sociolinguistic competence).</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new vocabulary word I feel confident using when talking about future jobs is: _____."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I will practice to improve my speaking about future plans is: _____."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did practicing the job interview role-play help you feel more confident explaining your future career plans?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What challenges did you face when talking about the future job market, and how will you overcome them?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The speaking activities effectively engaged students in using future tenses and modals. The job interview role-play allowed for creative application of vocabulary and grammar. Some students struggled with differentiating /dʒ/ and /ʒ/ consistently, and some needed more prompting to use future continuous. Next time, I will provide more explicit pronunciation drills before the role-play and more sentence starters for the future continuous tense during practice.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking practice of explaining future plans and job requirements will directly feed into Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), where students will write a short cover letter or a personal statement outlining their career aspirations and the skills they aim to acquire for a specific "job of the future."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>